 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 42,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "PULTENEY BRIDGE. Robert Adam, 1774. One of very few bridges in the world lined with shops. Wander over and buy a fudge."
  },
  {
   "x": 8,
   "y": 14,
   "text": "THE ROYAL CRESCENT. 30 houses in one sweeping curve, 1767 to 1774. The lawn drops away in a ha-ha, a hidden ditch that keeps the cows off without spoiling the view."
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "BATH ABBEY. Founded 757, rebuilt in the 1500s. 'The Lantern of the West' for its huge windows. The ladders carved on the front come from a bishop's dream of angels."
  },
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 31,
   "text": "THE ROMAN BATHS. Sulis Minerva's temple, nearly 2,000 years old. About a million litres of 46 degree spring water a day. Please do not drink the green one."
  },
  {
   "x": 56,
   "y": 26,
   "text": "THERMAE BATH SPA. A rooftop pool of natural hot spring water at about 33 degrees, open to the sky. Britain's only naturally hot springs rise right here in Bath."
  },
  {
   "x": 23,
   "y": 37,
   "text": "VICTORIA PARK. Opened in 1830 by the 11-year-old Princess Victoria. She was not allowed to run. You are."
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 38,
   "text": "BATH'S HOT SPRINGS. Britain's only natural ones, three of them, rising at the Roman Baths. Cold water from every other tap. Be grateful."
  }
 ],
